Head-tracked stereoscopic 3D: let the fold3ds layer supply the render Surface.

BEN-APPROVED AZAHAR SOURCE CHANGE. Asked directly and answered 2026-09-25,
relayed by CEO Boss: "Route C. The one-line change in EmulationFragment so it
accepts a Surface we supply. It goes in patches/, marked as Ben-approved, in the
Android layer only."

It is kept in its own patch file, separate from azahar-fold3ds.patch, so that the
one approved change to Azahar's own rendering path stays auditable on its own and
Bug Checker Dev's upstream diff can record it as expected rather than as drift.

WHAT IT DOES

Two lines, both in the Android layer, no C++ and no renderer code. Azahar's
`surfaceChanged` handed native the SurfaceView's Surface directly; it now passes
that Surface through `Fold3dsStereo.wrap`, which returns:

  * stereo off -- the very same Surface object, so the code path is byte-for-byte
    upstream behaviour and the feature cannot regress normal 2D play;
  * stereo on  -- a SurfaceTexture-backed Surface at the same size. Azahar
    renders its side-by-side pair (`render_3d` = 1 or 2) into that texture, and
    our GL pass composites it onto the real Surface with eye separation and
    convergence driven by the front camera's head position.

`surfaceDestroyed` releases the compositor before native drops the surface,
because the EGL context and the SurfaceTexture belong to the target Surface and
outliving it leaks both.

WHY IT CANNOT BE DONE WITHOUT TOUCHING THIS FILE

`NativeLibrary.surfaceChanged(surf: Surface)` accepts any Surface, so the native
side needs nothing. But the only caller is this fragment, inside Azahar's own
EmulationActivity, which the fold3ds layer launches by Intent
(Fold3dsLinkActivity.kt). The alternative was to host emulation ourselves and
call NativeLibrary.run/surfaceChanged directly -- no Azahar change, but it
reimplements this fragment's lifecycle, input and save-state handling. Ben chose
the two lines.

WHAT THIS IS NOT

It is not look-around parallax, and Ben was told so before approving. Azahar
renders exactly two views at a baseline fixed by `factor_3d`; two images with no
depth buffer cannot be turned into new viewpoints. The head drives separation,
convergence and framing -- convincing on the device, but not motion parallax.

diff --git a/src/android/app/src/main/java/org/citra/citra_emu/fragments/EmulationFragment.kt b/src/android/app/src/main/java/org/citra/citra_emu/fragments/EmulationFragment.kt
--- a/src/android/app/src/main/java/org/citra/citra_emu/fragments/EmulationFragment.kt
+++ b/src/android/app/src/main/java/org/citra/citra_emu/fragments/EmulationFragment.kt
@@ -1635,10 +1635,11 @@ class EmulationFragment :

     override fun surfaceChanged(holder: SurfaceHolder, format: Int, width: Int, height: Int) {
         Log.debug("[EmulationFragment] Surface changed. Resolution: " + width + "x" + height)
-        emulationState.newSurface(holder.surface)
+        emulationState.newSurface(org.citra.citra_emu.fold3ds.Fold3dsStereo.wrap(holder.surface, width, height))
     }

     override fun surfaceDestroyed(holder: SurfaceHolder) {
+        org.citra.citra_emu.fold3ds.Fold3dsStereo.release()
         emulationState.clearSurface()
     }

